## What a Stage Monitor Is, and What It Is Not

<InlineCTA />

A stage monitor, also called a floor wedge or monitor wedge, is a speaker angled up at a performer or speaker standing on stage, so they can hear themselves and each other over the room mix, which is a completely different signal path from the PA speakers aimed out at the audience. That distinction matters because the active PA speakers built into the Eco Pack, Wedding Pack, and MICE Pack, covered in our [audio visual rental](/blog/audio-visual-rental/) guide and our sound system rental guide, are an audience facing product. A stage monitor faces the opposite direction and answers a different need: what the person on stage hears, not what the crowd hears.

It is also worth ruling out a second, unrelated meaning some searches land on by accident: a "monitor" in an AV context can also mean a video screen a presenter reads from, sometimes called a confidence monitor. That is a display, not a speaker, and it is a different rental entirely from what this page covers. See the FAQ on this below if that is actually what you are searching for.

## Passive Wedges and the Amplifiers That Power Them

Here is the detail that matters most and that some local listings get wrong: our HK Audio PR:O 10-X monitors are passive, not self powered. A passive wedge has no built in amplifier, it needs a dedicated external amp channel to drive it, which is exactly what we pair it with: 2 MC2-ETAPA-T-1000 amplifiers, 500 watts per unit across 2 channels, and 1 MC2-T-2000, 1,000 watts across 2 channels, all from our own inventory.

We are stating this directly because at least one other Malaga rental company advertises stage monitors as self amplified, which they are not, at least not the passive category product this page is describing. A passive wedge run off a dedicated amp channel is not a downgrade from a self powered unit, it is the setup larger venues and touring productions run as standard, operated by a sound engineer working from the same amp rack as the rest of the system, rather than a string of individually powered boxes each running its own internal electronics. That is also how it fits into a Malaga Event Gear booking: through the same technician managed signal chain as the rest of your sound system, not as a stand alone plug in unit.

## Is a Stage Monitor Bundled Into Any Package?

No. None of our 5 standard packages, the Eco Pack, Wedding Pack, Basic MICE Pack, MICE Pack, or Product Presentation Pack, list a stage monitor in their includes. Their sound equipment is built around active PA speakers aimed at the audience, not floor wedges aimed at performers, because most events booking those packages do not have a band or a panel of speakers on a raised stage needing their own monitor mix.

If your event does, [contact us](/contact/) with your stage plot, how many performers or speakers need their own wedge, and which package you are otherwise booking, and we will quote the monitor rental as an add on rather than try to force it into a package that was never built to include it.

## How Many Stage Monitors Does a Band or Panel Need?

It depends on your lineup, but here is the honest ceiling: our own inventory holds 8 HK Audio PR:O 10-X wedges, so that is the real maximum available for a single booking, not an inflated number pulled from a manufacturer's full catalog. A small acoustic act or a 2 or 3 person panel typically needs 1 to 3 wedges, a full band with a drummer, vocalist, and multiple instrumentalists is often 4 to 6, and a larger stage with multiple speakers or performers can use up toward our full stock of 8. Tell us your stage plot and we will confirm exact count and amplifier pairing before your date, rather than after.

## Self Run or Technician Managed Monitor Mix

A monitor mix, how loud each performer's wedge is and what is in it, is a live adjustment, not a one time setting, which is why we pair stage monitor rental with a technician on larger bookings rather than leaving it self run by default. If your event is already booking the [Wedding Pack](/packages/wedding/) or [MICE Pack](/packages/mice/), a live technician is already included and can manage the monitor mix alongside the rest of the sound system for the duration of your event. On a self run booking like the [Eco Pack](/packages/eco/), a band typically manages their own monitor levels the same way they would run their own mixer, which our audio visual rental for music performances guide covers for that self run context specifically. Either way, [contact us](/contact/) with how your stage is staffed so we can quote the right setup.

